<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-06.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="002405"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002405" label="[00:24:05]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what do you mean?</span></div><div class="line" id="002436"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002436" label="[00:24:36]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pijul just like darcs is patch-based version control, while fossil, git, cvs and others are snapshot-based</span></div><div class="line" id="002445"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002445" label="[00:24:45]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">radicle uses git, so is not patch-based</span></div><div class="line" id="003656"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003656" label="[00:36:56]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in terms of bootstrap. the http downloads are binary-only. the source is only on radicle, or (hopefully the same data) on cargo.</span></div><div class="line" id="004049"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004049" label="[00:40:49]">&lt;pabs3&gt;</a></span><span class="message">PS: Rust in QEMU Roadmap <a rel="nofollow" href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/cc40943e-dec1-4890-a1d9-579350ce296f@pbonzini.local/">https://lore.kernel.org/all/cc40943e-dec1-4890-a1d9-579350ce296f@pbonzini.local/</a>  <a rel="nofollow" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42253814">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=42253814</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="005536"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005536" label="[00:55:36]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess in a way rust is better than haskell in that it builds, but with haskell every update of ghc breaks compatibility and everything has to be adjusted, furthermore many haskell libraries break compatibility every update and everything has to be adjusted again</span></div><div class="line" id="005953"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005953" label="[00:59:53]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but talking about that on #haskell leads to response that those breakages are always for improvement and it's easy to adjust existing projects when breakages happen</span></div><div class="line" id="010529"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#010529" label="[01:05:29]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">:\</span></div><div class="line" id="010602"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#010602" label="[01:06:02]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm pretty sure that rust at least has a policy that release 1.X builds with 1.(X-1)</span></div><div class="line" id="010621"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#010621" label="[01:06:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, but that's usually the only one that builds it</span></div><div class="line" id="010623"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#010623" label="[01:06:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and 1.X itself</span></div><div class="line" id="010634"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#010634" label="[01:06:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">often evne 1.(X+1) wouldn't work</span></div><div class="line" id="010642"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#010642" label="[01:06:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i.e. you can't downgrade</span></div><div class="line" id="011257"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011257" label="[01:12:57]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes</span></div><div class="line" id="011309"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011309" label="[01:13:09]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but haskell has X.Y may or may not build with X.(Y-1)</span></div><div class="line" id="011311"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011311" label="[01:13:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lol</span></div><div class="line" id="011602"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#011602" label="[01:16:02]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if only there was another functional language...</span></div><div class="line" id="011603"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#011603" label="[01:16:03]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: well if we can bootstrap GHC on *ANY* architecture, other architectures can do QEMU builds of GHC bootstrap and then just do a cross compile to the native architecture.</span></div><div class="line" id="011722"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#011722" label="[01:17:22]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so getting a complete as possible chain in Guix gives us a way to document and preserve a chain we can link to later</span></div><div class="line" id="011743"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#011743" label="[01:17:43]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Although it seems like qemu wants to adopt rust… :(</span></div><div class="line" id="011811"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#011811" label="[01:18:11]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unfortunately everything nowadays wants to adopt rust</span></div><div class="line" id="011813"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#011813" label="[01:18:13]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then a bootstrap Haskell compiler only needs to target the first link in the chain (which someone could do simplification on incrementally)</span></div><div class="line" id="011844"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#011844" label="[01:18:44]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: well, atleast we have a clean bootstrap path for Rust from GCC</span></div><div class="line" id="011911"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011911" label="[01:19:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and QEMU is also a fairly complete piece of software, i don't see using today's QEMU being a problem in 10 years</span></div><div class="line" id="011911"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#011911" label="[01:19:11]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I tried programming in rust, I don't understand why would anyone program in that language, but more and more projects are switching to rust</span></div><div class="line" id="011925"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011925" label="[01:19:25]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lol homo, what's your biggest pain point?</span></div><div class="line" id="011937"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#011937" label="[01:19:37]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">verbosity</span></div><div class="line" id="011939"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011939" label="[01:19:39]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm not a rust lover, but not a hater either</span></div><div class="line" id="011941"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011941" label="[01:19:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yea fair</span></div><div class="line" id="011945"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#011945" label="[01:19:45]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">via gccrs or rustc-gcc-backend (not sure on the name of this one)? Last time I checked they didn't work for bootstrapping but it's been a while.</span></div><div class="line" id="011957"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#011957" label="[01:19:57]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: via mrustc</span></div><div class="line" id="012026"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#012026" label="[01:20:26]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">one possible solution for the GHC bootstrap is to take the simplest version of GHC we can chain and remove the use of one haskell feature at a time until it can be built by a Haskell98 compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="012027"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#012027" label="[01:20:27]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah yeah right, that's an extra step, but one that at least currently works</span></div><div class="line" id="012213"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#012213" label="[01:22:13]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we don't have to like what programming languages other people use. We just have to bootstrap and have bootstrap chains for those languages and then we can let the Guix community do the long term support for those languages.</span></div><div class="line" id="012315"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#012315" label="[01:23:15]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">haskell98 doesn't have FFI, pattern guards and hierarchical modules (Data.Char vs Char), so haskell2010 is better</span></div><div class="line" id="012422"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#012422" label="[01:24:22]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: well honestly, as long as someone moves the needle in the correct direction of finally getting a GHC bootstrap. I'll be happy to see progress.</span></div><div class="line" id="012521"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#012521" label="[01:25:21]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: &quot;better&quot; in what sense? as in an overall better language?</span></div><div class="line" id="012647"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#012647" label="[01:26:47]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: reading haskell report that is the only addition, but (n+k) patterns are removed from language, so yes, a better overall language</span></div><div class="line" id="013006"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#013006" label="[01:30:06]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: ummm, <a rel="nofollow" href="https://wiki.haskell.org/Implementations">https://wiki.haskell.org/Implementations</a>  they all appear to be written in Haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="013107"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013107" label="[01:31:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">absoltuely agree that it is a better language to program in, but the added complexity makes it an unideal target to directly bootstrap, assuming we can bootstrap haskell2010 from haskell98</span></div><div class="line" id="013235"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#013235" label="[01:32:35]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which might not be an issue if one of them was written in a smaller haskell subset, which could give us a path out</span></div><div class="line" id="013242"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013242" label="[01:32:42]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, do you really want to convert hierarchical modules to non-hierarchical like renaming Data.Char to Datachar? and what about FFI? ghc is not written entirely in haskell</span></div><div class="line" id="013338"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013338" label="[01:33:38]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: that's what basically blynn's compiler is about? small subset to implement a bigger subset until a good enough subset is implemented?</span></div><div class="line" id="013418"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013418" label="[01:34:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm confused. why do we want to do that? the most promising method at the moment seems to be soemthing -&gt; something else -&gt; ancient GHC (0.26??) -&gt; marginally newer GHC -&gt; marginally newer GHC -&gt; ....... -&gt; modern GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="013429"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013429" label="[01:34:29]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and somewhere in those ......, conversions from haskell98 to haskell2010 occur</span></div><div class="line" id="013520"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#013520" label="[01:35:20]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: kinda and we also could do the reverse with GHC until we can get a simpler Haskell compiler able to build that extended chain.</span></div><div class="line" id="013525"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013525" label="[01:35:25]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: do you have hardware that is capable to build so many versions of ghc in less than 2 months?</span></div><div class="line" id="013536"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013536" label="[01:35:36]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: no idea, but i suspect so</span></div><div class="line" id="013701"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013701" label="[01:37:01]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm actually afraid to even try building a single version of ghc simply because of how much resources it eats, I'd rather rely on compiler that implements good enough subset of haskell2010</span></div><div class="line" id="013704"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#013704" label="[01:37:04]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bootstrappable Haskell -&gt; better haskell -&gt; bootstrap achieved &lt;- simpler haskell able to build ghc -1 &lt;- simpler haskell able to build ghc &lt;- ghc</span></div><div class="line" id="013840"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013840" label="[01:38:40]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">when you say &quot;good enough&quot;, do you mean for general Haskell programming, or to build GHC?</span></div><div class="line" id="013901"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013901" label="[01:39:01]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">FWIW, on my Gentoo system, rust takes ~1 hr to build, GHC takes around the same amount</span></div><div class="line" id="014138"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014138" label="[01:41:38]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: for general haskell programming, the subset I use while programming, but again I'd be happier programming in different functional language that has nice-looking syntax, lazy evaluation, strong type system and IO monad</span></div><div class="line" id="014323"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014323" label="[01:43:23]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I tried building rust once, it took many hours, and my current laptop is not any better</span></div><div class="line" id="014418"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014418" label="[01:44:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yea gotcha</span></div><div class="line" id="014626"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014626" label="[01:46:26]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I like the approach of go, they don't have feature creep in the language, only basic features that everyone uses while programming</span></div><div class="line" id="020959"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#020959" label="[02:09:59]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; homo: the problem is, &quot;Haskell 2010&quot; is a paper language</span></div><div class="line" id="021012"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021012" label="[02:10:12]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; No program was ever written it</span></div><div class="line" id="021043"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021043" label="[02:10:43]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; By the time the 2010 standard was published, everyone was treating &quot;Haskell&quot; as a synonym for &quot;whatever GHC will accept&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="021110"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021110" label="[02:11:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; In particular, there's no Haskell 2010 implementation written in Haskell 2010</span></div><div class="line" id="021235"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021235" label="[02:12:35]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; What makes nhc98 interesting for bootstrapping is that it implements Haskell98 (&amp; some extensions), and is written in Haskell98 (possibly with some use of extensions)</span></div><div class="line" id="021310"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021310" label="[02:13:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Hugs is interesting because it's written in an eager language, and it implements Haskell98-ish</span></div><div class="line" id="021341"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021341" label="[02:13:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; (unfortunately not full Haskell98, and certainly not whatever is required to build the first Haskell98-compliant GHC)</span></div><div class="line" id="021422"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021422" label="[02:14:22]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Yale Haskell is interesting because it, too, is written in an eager language, and it implements Haskell 1.3 (-ish? it's not clear, unfortunately)</span></div><div class="line" id="021454"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021454" label="[02:14:54]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Hugs &amp; Yale Haskell both make the jump from an eager language to a real-life lazy one</span></div><div class="line" id="021517"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#021517" label="[02:15:17]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; Blynn &amp; microHs go from an eager language to a toy lazy one</span></div><div class="line" id="022146"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#022146" label="[02:21:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, I was building the whole rustc chain, it's probably 2 or 3 hours here if you do full LLVM + 3 stage rust build and then times 20 or so builds...</span></div><div class="line" id="022159"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#022159" label="[02:21:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though soon I guess we should be able to switch to mrustc  that can build 1.74</span></div><div class="line" id="023239"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023239" label="[02:32:39]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: what hardware do you have out of interest?</span></div><div class="line" id="023321"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023321" label="[02:33:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">starbook mkvi, so 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-1360P</span></div><div class="line" id="023538"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023538" label="[02:35:38]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gotcha, that explains things, cause I was thinking you build at least the first bits of live-bootstrap faster than I do, but my rust build is faster than yours</span></div><div class="line" id="023600"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023600" label="[02:36:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you have amd system?</span></div><div class="line" id="023606"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023606" label="[02:36:06]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, 5700x</span></div><div class="line" id="023612"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023612" label="[02:36:12]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess it's more parallel but maybe slower single thread performance</span></div><div class="line" id="023637"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#023637" label="[02:36:37]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, probably</span></div><div class="line" id="023831"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#023831" label="[02:38:31]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">googulator: and also hugs is impossible to build on guix despite being packaged</span></div><div class="line" id="025131"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#025131" label="[02:51:31]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: sounds like a bug that just needs to be reported.</span></div><div class="line" id="083851"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#083851" label="[08:38:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; 5700x, being a desktop CPU, will also stay in turbo clocks for longer than a low-power notebook one like 1360P</span></div><div class="line" id="083913"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#083913" label="[08:39:13]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;googulator&gt; especially under multicore loads</span></div><br /></div></body></html>